    Starting TransAmigaConfig:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    TransAmigaConfig can be run from either the CLI or WorkBench. Start from the CLI simply by typing 'TransAmigaConfig' at the CLI prompt. Start from the WorkBench by clicking on the icon. There are no command line options or WorkBench tool types. There is one assignment that must be made prior to running TransAmigaConfig. The assignment is 'BBS:'. There should also be a directory called 'Configs' in the BBS: directory. This is not necessary though.


    Program Operation:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    When TransAmigaConfig starts up, an ASL file requester pops up on the WorkBench screen asking for the 'Main TransAmiga BBS configuration file'.  The main configuration file is usually called 'TransAmiga.cfg'. If the configuration file does not exist, TransAmigaConfig will create the file for you putting in some default values. After this is done, there will be a window on your WorkBench screen with the options that you choose.

    The following describes the operation of each function in TransAmigaConfig.


    System/Paths:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    When this button is clicked, another window will open with the current system definitions and the paths to the various directories that TransAmiga BBS requires.


    Modem:
    ~~~~~~
    When this button is clicked, another window will open containing all the modem options for TransAmiga BBS.


    New User:
    ~~~~~~~~~
    This option will allow you to configure the values given to a new user to your system.


    Screen/Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This option will open a window to allow you to configure the screen and window options for TransAmiga BBS.


    Guest User:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~
    A window will open allowing you to configure the values given to a guest user t your BBS.


    File Areas:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~
    This is one of the more powerful features of TransAmigaConfig. It will use the path given in the System/Paths string gadget call 'Config Path', to open the Files.cfg configuration file used by TransAmiga BBS. A window will open allowing you to fully configure your file areas.


    Message Areas:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Again this will use the path given in the System/Paths string gadget called 'Config Path', to open the Message.cfg configuration file used by TransAmiga BBS. A window will open allowing you to fully configure your message areas.


    Save Master:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Clicking on this button will save the current main configuration file in memory to disk. It will be the same name as selected by the original ASL file requester at the start of the program.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~
    This is how to exit the program. Clicking this button will exit. NOTE: If you have made changes to the main configuration and have not clicked on the save button, all the changes will be lost. Clicking on the 'Close box' will also terminate the program without saving any changes.



    The System/Paths Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    System:
    ~~~~~~~

    BBS Name:
    ~~~~~~~~~
    Use this gadget to enter the name of your BBS.


    Sysop Name:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~
    Use this gadget to enter your name as the sysop of the BBS.


    Priv. Access:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Privileged access. This sets the privileged access for your system.


    Idle Time:
    ~~~~~~~~~~

    This sets the idle time of the BBS before disconnecting.


    Log Level:
    ~~~~~~~~~~

    This slider gadget controls the log level for your BBS.


    Paths:
    ~~~~~~

    System:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the main system path for TransAmiga BBS.


    Config. Files:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the path to the configuration files for TransAmiga BBS. NOTE: This is the path used by TransAmigaConfig to open the Files.cfg and Message.cfg configuration files. Make sure they are correct before selecting the File Areas or Message Areas options.


    User Files:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the path to the user files for the BBS.


    Text:
    ~~~~~

    This is the path to the text files.


    Bulletin:
    ~~~~~~~~~

    This is the path to the bulletins used by TransAmiga BBS.


    Extras:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the path to the extras, or doors area for the BBS.


    Arexx:
    ~~~~~~

    This is the path to the arexx programs used by TransAmiga.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This will take you back to the main option screen.



    The Modem Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Serial:
    ~~~~~~~

    Device:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the serial device used by TransAmiga.


    Unit:
    ~~~~~

    The unit number of the serial device.


    Min. Baud Rate:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Enter the minimum baud rate you wish callers to have to log on to your BBS.


    Max. Baud Rate:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Enter the maximum baud rate your modem supports.


    Modem Strings:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Initialization:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the initialization string sent to the modem when the BBS starts up.


    Exit:
    ~~~~~

    This is the string sent to the modem when exiting TransAmiga.


    Local LogOn:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This string is sent to the modem when you do a local logon to TransAmiga BBS.


    Local LogOff:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This string is sent to the modem when you are finished a TransAmiga local session.


    Answer:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the string sent to the modem to answer when you are not using another program to answer the phone.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This will take you back to the main options window.



    The New User Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Access:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the access level given to new, validated users.


    Time Limit:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the time limit given to new users.


    File Ratio:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the file ratio you wish to give to new users to your system.


    Byte Ratio:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the byte ratio you wish to give to new users to your system.


    Flags:
    ~~~~~~

    This series of 8 checkboxes select the flags you wish to give a new user to your system. The boxes are in the same order as the 'X--X---X' used in TransConfig. Checking a box sets the flag to a 'X', a non-checked box will set the flag to a '-'.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This takes you back to the main options window.



    The Screen/Window Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Screen:
    ~~~~~~~

    Type:
    ~~~~~

    This cycle gadget allows you to select the screen mode TransAmiga uses. If the screen mode is set to WorkBench, the other gadgets in the Screen section will be disabled.


    Bit Planes:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    Enter the number of bitplanes you wish to use on a custom screen.


    Blue Backdrop:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Setting the checkbox here will select the blue backdrop window option for the custom screen.


    Width & Height:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Enter the width and height of the custom screen in these gadgets.


    Terminal Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    TopX TopY Width Height:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Enter the top left of your terminal window and the width and height of the terminal window in these gadgets.


    Font Name:
    ~~~~~~~~~~

    Enter the name of the font you want TransAmiga BBS to use in the terminal window.


    Size:
    ~~~~~

    This is the size of the font to be used.


    Sizing Gadget:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Check this box if you wish a sizing gadget to be present on the terminal window.


    Prompt Field:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Check this box if you want callers to your BBS to be displayed a prompt field on their terminal.


    Color:
    ~~~~~~

    Input Prompt Trim:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    These are the color numbers you wish to use for these types of text.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This will take you back to the main options window.



    The Guest User Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Time Limit:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the time limit given to users that have not joined your BBS.


    File Ratio:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the file ratio given to guest users.


    Byte Ratio:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the byte ratio given to guest users.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This will take you back to the main options window.



    The File Areas Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Areas Listview Gadget:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This listview gadget contains all the file area names that you choose for your BBS. To add an area just click on the 'Add' button under the listview. A window will open allowing you to enter the information required for the area. To edit or delete an area, you must select the area by pointing the mouse to the area name in the listview and clicking the left mouse button. The name of the selected area will appear in the window below the listview gadget.


    Archivers Listview Gadget:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    The operation of this listview is the same as for the Areas listview. To add an archiver, click on the 'Add' button below the archivers listview. To edit or delete an archiver, the archiver must first be selected.


    Protocalls Listview Gadget:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Again this listview operates the same as for the Areas and Archivers listview gadgets. Use the buttons below the protocall listview to perform the 'Add', 'Edit' and 'Delete' functions.


    Min. Free KB:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the minimum free disk space that must be on your drive in order to allow uploads to your system.


    Max. Marked Files:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the maximum amount of files a user may mark for batch downloading.


    Save:
    ~~~~~

    This will save any changes to the Files.cfg configuration file. NOTE: If this gadget is not used, exiting the file areas section will result in the loss of any changes.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This will take you back to the main options window. Remember that if any changes have been made, they will be lost it you do not click the save button.



    The Add/Edit File Area Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This window opens when a file area is being added or edited.


    Area #:
    ~~~~~~~

    This text gadget shows which area number is currently being added or edited.


    Area:
    ~~~~~

    Name:
    ~~~~~

    This is the name of the file area that is shown to users to your BBS.


    Path:
    ~~~~~

    This is the physical path to the area on your disk.


    Access:
    ~~~~~~~

    Library:
    ~~~~~~~~

    This is the minimum access level a user must have to enter the file area.


    Read:
    ~~~~~

    This is the minimum access level a user must have to download files in this file area.


    Write:
    ~~~~~~

    This is the minimum access level a user must have to upload files to this area.


    Flags:
    ~~~~~~

    These series of 8 checkboxes are the flags a user must have to gain access to this file area. They are in the same order as the 'X---X-XX' format used in the TransConfig program. The left checkbox represents the first 'X'.


    Cancel:
    ~~~~~~~

    This button cancels the Add/Edit of the file area making no changes.


    Done:
    ~~~~~

    This button saves the changes to the file areas in memory. No permanent changes are made unless the 'Save' button is pressed in the main file areas window.



    The Add/Edit Archiver Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Name:
    ~~~~~
    This is the name of the archiver.


    Suffix:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the filename suffix the archiver uses. eg (.lha).


    Command:
    ~~~~~~~~

    The command used to list the contents of an archived file.


    Cancel:
    ~~~~~~~

    This button cancels the Add/Edit of an archiver. No changes are made.


    Done:
    ~~~~~

    This button save any changes to memory. No permanent changes are made until the save button is pressed in the main file areas window.



    The Add/Edit Protocall Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Name:
    ~~~~~

    This is the name of the transfer protocall.


    Send Command:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the command used to send a user a file.


    Receive Command:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the command used to receive a file from a user.


    Type:
    ~~~~~

    This cycle gadget selects the type of transfer. Either batch or normal (single file).


    Cancel:
    ~~~~~~~

    This button will cancel the Add/Edit function without making any changes.


    Done:
    ~~~~~

    This button will save the changes to memory. No permanent changes will be made until the save button is pressed in the main file areas window.




    The Message Areas Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    The Message Areas Listview:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This listview gadget shows all the current message areas. Use the buttons below to 'Add', 'Edit' or 'Delete' a message area. Note: in order to edit or delete an area, it must first be selected as in the file areas window.


    The Fidonet Addresses Listview:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This listview gadget shows all the current fidonet addresses you have. Again use the buttons below to 'Add' or 'Delete' an address. Note: in order to delete an address, it first must be selected as in the file areas window.


    Paths:
    ~~~~~~

    Inbound:
    ~~~~~~~~

    This is the physical path to your directory that the inbound mail goes to.


    OutBound:
    ~~~~~~~~~

    This is the physical path to your directory that the outbound mail is in.


    Nodelist:
    ~~~~~~~~~

    For nodelist support, this is the path to that directory.


    Auto Switch Message Areas:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Check this box if you wish your users to be able to automatically switch message areas.


    Global Handles:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Click this box to globally disable the use of handles (aliases) on your system.


    Send FB To:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the user name you wish feedback to go to when the send feedback command is performed.


    Area #:
    ~~~~~~~

    This is the area number you want the feedback to be placed into.


    Local Editor Command:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the command used to run a local editor when a user is logged on locally.


    Remote FSE Editor Command:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the full screen editor command that is used by a user when logged on remotely.


    Save:
    ~~~~~

    Pressing this button will permanently save all changes made to the Message.cfg configuration file.


    Quit:
    ~~~~~

    This will take you back to the main options window. NOTE: if changes have been made and the save button has not been clicked on, all changes will be lost.



    The Add/Edit Message Area Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Area #:
    ~~~~~~~

    This text gadget shows what message area is currently being added or edited.


    Area:
    ~~~~~

    Name:
    ~~~~~

    This is the name of the message area shown to users to your BBS.


    Path:
    ~~~~~

    This is the physical path to the directory that the messages are saved in.


    Access:
    ~~~~~~~

    Conference:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the minimum access level a user must have to use the message area.


    Read:
    ~~~~~

    This is the minimum access level a user must have to read messages in this area.


    Write:
    ~~~~~~

    This is the minimum access level a user must have to write messages in this area.


    Type:
    ~~~~~

    This cycle gadget selects the type of the message area. 'Private', 'Local', 'NetMail' or 'Echo'.


    Allow Handles:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    Checking this box will allow users to uses handles (aliases) in this message area.


    Quote Line:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the quote lead in line used for this message area.


    Fido Address Num.:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the fidonet address number used for this message area if it is an echo or netmail.


    Origin:
    ~~~~~~~

    For a fidonet echo area this is the origin line used at the end of the message.


    Cancel:
    ~~~~~~~

    This will cancel the add/edit operation without making any changes.


    Done:
    ~~~~~

    This will make changes to the message area in memory only. No permanent changes will be made unless the save button is pressed in the main message areas window.



    The Add Fidonet Address Window:
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


    Address #:
    ~~~~~~~~~~

    This is the number of the fidonet address that you are adding.


    Domain:
    ~~~~~~~

    Enter you fidonet domain here.


    Zone:
    ~~~~~

    Enter your fidonet zone number here.


    Net:
    ~~~~

    Enter your fidonet net number here.


    Node:
    ~~~~~

    Enter your fidonet node number here.


    Point:
    ~~~~~~

    Enter your fidonet point number here.


    PointNet:
    ~~~~~~~~~

    If you operate a pointnet, enter the pointnet number here.


    Cancel:
    ~~~~~~~

    This cancels the operation without making any changes.


    Done:
    ~~~~~

    This will save the added fidonet address to memory. No permanent changes will be made unless the save button has been pressed in the main message areas window.



